hrough  school  it has now been five years since they  first spoke  and anderson and adams are still in  touch over facetime the kind of love he  showed me  you know the things he sent me you know  the money and the care of love you know  my mom and everything the love he showed  me it's overwhelming prince anderson is  now 25  stephen ira adams is 41 and he's a dad  to two boys  he told us he also sees himself as a  father figure to prince i told him to  quit that call center and he never went  back  because he found out what it was about  some of the people working there i found  out don't even know what's going on  his next job after he quit that call  center he was washing windows at the  airport as of today prince anderson is  now working for a private security  company in the capital of ghana  and in a twist of fate his brother isaac  anderson got a scholarship to howard  university in washington dc where he  recently started his freshman year  and when the dorms closed for winter  break  adams invited isaac to spend christmas  with him and the family in alexandria  louisiana how do you refer to isaac  isaac sometimes i say son no say my dad  yeah you call him your dad yeah i seem  to be my father because  the kind of things he's doing for me  care and all that  he's a tough man like really top a tough  man  they may look like an unlikely family  but adam said that he learned early on  as a boy about the enduring power of  kindness  coming from strangers from where i came  from in life to where i'm at now is a  journey as well tell me about where you  came from uh i was uh born to very young  parents he revealed to us details about  a tumultuous childhood that he says  included abuse abandonment and poverty  we're not going to make it by ourselves  in this world you know especially at  that young age there was a restaurant  beside us and when they were closing  dumping the stuff out oh i went over  there i was like i'll take them potato  logs  the people at the restaurant found out  that we were there by ourselves you know  nobody called the authorities we had a  neighbor an older lady that was a  neighbor she'd kind of check in on us so  i learned how to survive there  he wound up in foster care for a brief  period  and then was raised by his grandmother  and his grandfather who was a u.s air  force veteran  and he says his grandfather instilled in  him a profound love of country  and he says that's what led him to serve  his country himself you've experienced  abandonment  abuse poverty  food deprivation  yes i have  yep yep but i had the opportunity to  pull myself out of it in the united  states of america though and you said  you've always been a caretaker yes  because you had to take care of yourself  take care of that for so long yeah yeah  and then my younger siblings as well so  that's where this goodness comes from  it comes from pain it comes from pain it  comes from being coming from pain yeah  absolutely right you've been searching  all your life  because you've been looking for  consistency you've been looking for  stability stability you have been  looking for stability absolutely yep i  think about when i was younger the  different random people that helped me  everything from neighbors to people i  met on my bicycle to the janitors at my  high school yeah to the lunchroom lady  gave me some extra food to bring home  with me because you had to rely on  everybody yeah yeah i did and i wanted  to be reliable to them too  oh he is reliable  and ready to answer a call  no matter who's calling  or where it's coming from you never know  who you're going to be able to help out  there if you're just open to it we are  our brother's keepers it's our  responsibility to make sure  you know our neighbors taking care of  our children our family and if we all do  that for each other then we're going to  be fine i think what the world is  missing is love  oh my gosh david i'm thinking i need to  come lay on your couch because that's  right i love the exchange that you had  between you and stephen and there was  also something very touched by stephen  just when he said that he could hear  something in his voice that he knew that  he could intuitively pick up on that i  like what the brother said stephen is  indeed a top man don't you think he's a  top man yeah david that was incredible  interview it was almost like he was  discovering along with you what was  really behind this incredible act and  how he's led his life  you know tony it was a privilege to sit  there and listen to his story because i  always like to say that we as humans  connect through weaknesses right i can't  relate to talking to a scammer and  saying hey call me back but i can relate  to pain and suddenly that story became  real relatable to  to the audience and even my my team in  the room watching i i'd love to tell you  what the boys want to do by the way so  prince who's still living in ghana wants  to come to louisiana study criminal  justice and actually work as a police  officer in the area where steven adams  and his family live isaac the brother  who spent christmas with the adams  family he's still at howard he's having  a little trouble paying the meal plan so  he may have to dump out of howard and  he's planning to potentially move to  louisiana and enroll in college  here  david what i love about your pieces like  gil said they turn into therapy sessions  and  one thing i took away from that is your  prior pain if channeled properly can  turn into something special a gift for  someone else the if the if is the big  question david you talk to people in  this situation all the time and some  people turn it into love and other  people don't for some reason what do you  think that reason is what's the  difference maker  i think if you're able to and you're  willing to you start to feel the benefit  of what is that gift because to give  invariably is to get the joy that comes  from it and i think that's